"TabVLA: Targeted Backdoor Attacks on Vision-Language-Action Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10932v1)\nciteturn2728view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10932v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 404\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†I Introduction L17:   3. cite7†II Threat Model L18:     1. cite8†II-A Fine-tuning-time capabilities L19:     2. cite9†II-B Inference-time capabilities L20:   4. cite10†III Method Design L21:     1. cite11†III-A Preliminaries L22:     2. cite12†III-B Poisoned Dataset Construction L23:     3. cite13†III-C Fine-tuning of the Backdoored VLA L24:   5. cite14†IV Experiments L25:     1. cite15†IV-A Environmental Setup L26:     2. cite16†IV-B Implementation Details L27:     3. cite17†IV-C Main Results L28:     4. cite18†IV-D Ablation Studies L29:     5. cite19†IV-E Robustness to Trigger Mismatch L30:   6. cite20†V Discussion L31:   7. cite21†VI Potential Defense Against TabVLA L32:   8. cite22†VII Conclusion L33:   9. cite23†References L34: cite24†License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0†info.arxiv.org L35: \nL36: arXiv:2510.10932v1 [cs.CR] 13 Oct 2025\nL37: # TabVLA: Targeted Backdoor Attacks on Vision-Language-Action Models\nL38: Zonghuan Xu Affiliation:  Fudan University, Shanghai, China.    Xiang Zheng Affiliation:  City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China.    Xingjun Ma Affiliation:  Fudan University, Shanghai, China.    Yu-Gang Jiang ^{†}^{†}thanks: †˜Corresponding authors:xingjunma@fudan.edu.cn;xiang.zheng@cityu.edu.hk.^{†}^{†}thanks: This work was partially supported by the Junzheng Program of Fudan University. Affiliation:  Fudan University, Shanghai, China.\nL39: ###### Abstract\nL40: With the growing deployment of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models in real-world embodied AI systems, their increasing vulnerability to backdoor attacks poses a serious safety threat. A backdoored VLA agent can be covertly triggered by a pre-injected backdoor to execute adversarial actions, potentially causing system failures or even physical harm.\nL41: Although backdoor attacks on VLA models have been explored, prior work has focused only on untargeted attacks, leaving the more practically threatening scenario of targeted manipulation unexamined. In this paper, we study targeted backdoor attacks on VLA models and introduce TabVLA, a novel framework that enables such attacks via black-box fine-tuning. TabVLA explores two deployment-relevant inference-time threat models: input-stream editing and in-scene triggering.\nL42: It formulates poisoned data generation as an optimization problem to improve attack effectivess. Experiments with OpenVLA-7B on the LIBERO benchmark reveal that the vision channel is the principal attack surface: targeted backdoors succeed with minimal poisoning, remain robust across variations in trigger design, and are degraded only by positional mismatches between fine-tuning and inference triggers.\nL43: We also investigate a potential detection-based defense against TabVLA, which reconstructs latent visual triggers from the input stream to flag activation-conditioned backdoor samples. Our work highlights the vulnerability of VLA models to targeted backdoor manipulation and underscores the need for more advanced defenses.\nL44: ## I Introduction\nL45: As embodied AI systems (e.g., autonomous vehicles and household robots) move rapidly from prototypes to real-world deployment, ensuring their safety becomes a pressing concern. A central architecture underlying these systems is the Vision–Language–Action (VLA) paradigm, where models translate natural-language instructions and visual observations into executable actions [cite25†1 , cite26†2 ]. Unlike software-only systems, failures in embodied settings can result in immediate physical harm [cite27†19 ].\nL46: Among safety threats, backdoor attacks pose a distinct risk: with only minimal poisoning, an adversary can embed covert behaviors that preserve normal performance on clean inputs yet reliably activate malicious actions when triggers appear at inference.\nL47: Backdoor attacks have been extensively studied in both vision and language domains, with triggers ranging from imperceptible perturbations and localized patches to rare tokens and semantically benign phrases [cite28†5 , cite29†6 , cite30†7 , cite31†8 , cite32†9 ].\nL48: As research moves from static prediction to sequential decision-making, such as reinforcement learning (RL) and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), the attack surface expands beyond raw inputs to include observations, rewards, and preference signals [cite33†10 , cite34†11 , cite35†12 , cite36†13 , cite37†14 ].\nL49: These risks are amplified in embodied AI systems, where VLA models tightly couple multimodal perception with physical actuation [cite38†17 , cite39†18 , cite40†15 , cite41†16 , cite25†1 , cite42†20 ], making safety failures potentially consequential in the real world [cite27†19 ]. In the current literature, BadVLA [cite43†3 ] is the first and only work to explore backdoor attacks on VLA models.\nL50: While it demonstrates successful distraction of the target model, the approach is restricted to untargeted attacks, lacks a formal modeling of inference-time attacker capabilities, and does not investigate potential defenses.\nL51: This work investigates the feasibility of targeted backdoor attacks on VLA policies under more realistic assumptions about fine-tuning and inference pipelines. In particular, we consider a black-box fine-tuning setting where an adversary can supply data for model adaptation but has no privileged access to model parameters or the training procedure. Under this assmption, we introduce a generic attack framework, TabVLA, for mounting targeted backdoor attacks on blackbox VLA models during fine-tuning.\nL52: TabVLA explores two practical inference-time threat models. The first is an Input-Stream Editing threat model, where the adversary intercepts and modifies the input-stream during execution, through either the vision or language channel. The second is an In-Scene Triggering threat model, which preserves the integrity of the input pipeline but introduces visual trigger objects directly into the environment observed by the agent.\nL53: It formulates the generation and injection of poisoned data that elicit attacker-specified behaviors as an optimization problem to improve attack effectivnesss. With TabVLA, we conduct comprehensive experiments on the LIBERO benchmark [cite25†1 , cite42†20 ] with the OpenVLA model, analyzing trigger modalities, trigger types, and critical hyperparameters, as well as evaluating robustness under trigger–mismatch conditions.\nL54: Our findings show that visual cues are the dominant attack surface in VLA models. Even with small poisoning budgets, strong targeted attacks can be induced without degrading clean-task performance, highlighting the practical severity of the threat. Ablation studies further demonstrate that the attack remains robust to variations in vision-trigger geometry and language-trigger design, requiring little tuning to sustain effectiveness.\nL55: Our robustness evaluation examines mismatches between fine-tuning and inference, simulating realistic deployment conditions where discrepancies may arise. We find that most mismatches, including differences in visual appearance, contextual elements, or instruction phrasing, have minimal impact on attack success. However, misalignment of trigger location between training and deployment substantially reduces effectiveness, underscoring the importance of spatial consistency for real-world viability.\nL56: These results define concrete risk surfaces for VLA-based embodied agents and motivate the development of deployment-aware defenses. As an initial exploration, we also propose a vision-only detection method agasint TabVLA, which reconstructs latent visual triggers from the input stream to flag activation-conditioned backdoor samples.\nL57: In summary, our main contributions are as follows:\nL58: \nL59:   * •\nL60: \nL61: We propose TabVLA, a generic framework for targeted backdoor attacks on black-box VLA-based control policies during fine-tuning. TabVLA encompasses two threat models—input-stream editing and in-scene triggering—and supports both uni- and multi-modal poisoning strategies.\nL62: \nL63:   * •\nL64: We empirically demonstrate the vulnerability of VLA models to visually triggered backdoor attacks. Our results show that TabVLA can reliably induce strong targeted behaviors with minimal poisoning and no loss in clean-task performance. We further conduct systematic ablation and robustness analyses, revealing a key deployment risk: attack success remains robust to most trigger variations but is sensitive to mismatches between fine-tuning and inference.\nL65: \nL66:   * •\nL67: We further explore defenses against targeted VLA backdoor attacks by proposing a vision-only detection method based on trigger inversion, which identifies activation-conditioned visual patterns in the input stream.\nL68: ## II Threat Model\nL69: We first define the threat model for our black-box targeted backdoor attack framework, TabVLA. The adversary’s goal is to implant a targeted backdoor during the fine-tuning stage of the victim VLA and subsequently activate it at inference time by inducing the model to perceive a trigger and execute an attacker-specified action.\nL70: The adversary’s knowledge of the victim VLA is limited to a black-box setting; specifically, the adversary has no access to the model’s architecture, parameters, or gradients, and can only manipulate the training data and input channels. We summarize the adversary’s capabilities as follows:\nL71: ### II-A Fine-tuning-time capabilities\nL72: \nL73: During fine-tuning, the adversary may contribute or alter a small subset of training data by embedding triggers and manipulating labels, while the remaining data remains untouched. This setup reflects realistic scenarios in collaborative or crowdsourced training pipelines, where data is sourced from partially untrusted contributors, third-party datasets, simulation logs, or remote collection nodes, and cannot be easily verified.\nL74: ### II-B Inference-time capabilities\nL75: \nL76: In embodied settings, targeted backdoor attacks require high temporal precision: the adversary can induce an attacker-specified unsafe behavior at an exact moment during task execution. To clarify this, we outline two adversary capabilities at inference time for the victim VLA.\nL77: Input-stream editing. In this setting, the attacker can directly intercept and modify input-stream of the robot in certain time steps. This allows the adversary to precisely and stealthily inject linguistic or visual triggers. For instance, the adversary may overlay a 5 px red dot at pixel coordinate (10,10) on the camera feed at $t{=}1.2$ s and simultaneously append a rare token such as [sudo] to the instruction string.\nL78: In-scene triggering. In this setting, the adversary cannot directly access the VLA’s input-stream and instead introduces physical triggers into the environment, which are then captured by the robot’s cameras. For example, when the robot is lifting an object, the adversary may place a red sticker or LED within the wrist camera’s view, thereby exposing the trigger to the vision channel.\nL79: Compared with input-stream editing, in-scene triggering is more practical since it requires fewer system privileges. However, because the adversary cannot precisely manipulate the input-stream, the placement and appearance of the visual trigger are less controllable than in input-stream editing. This threat model motivates the design of TabVLA, which aims to enable stealthy and effective targeted attacks under minimal assumptions and across both capability settings.\nL80: It also underscores the need to evaluate the robustness of visual triggers, which we will investigate in Section cite14†IV .\nL81: ## III Method Design\nL82: We now present TabVLA, a generic, model- and data-agnostic framework for crafting targeted backdoor attacks on black-box VLAs. To clarify the principles underlying our design, we first introduce the preliminaries of VLAs, followed by a detailed description of multimodal trigger construction (linguistic, visual, and joint).\nL83: Finally, we propose a novel poisoning strategy that enforces consistency across $K$-step windows by relabeling all subsequent steps once a trigger appears, thereby preventing contradictory training signals.\nL84: ### III-A Preliminaries\nL85: \nL86: Vision–Language models. A VLA-based control policy $f_{\\theta}$ maps the current visual observation, natural language instruction, and proprioceptive inputs to a sequence of actions. At each timestep $t$, the robot observes an image $v_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H\\times W\\times C}$, receives proprioceptive state $s_{t}$ (e.g., positions or gripper status), and samples an action $a_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ conditioned on $(v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}},s_{t})$, i.e.,\nL87:  | $$a_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}},s_{t}).$$  |  | (1)\nL88: \nL89: For instance, the action of OpenVLA is defined as\nL90: \nL91:  | $$a_{t}=\\big[\\Delta p_{x},\\Delta p_{y},\\Delta p_{z},\\Delta r_{x},\\Delta r_{y},\\Delta r_{z},G\\big]^{\\top},$$  |  | (2)\nL92: \nL93: where $\\Delta p_{\\cdot}$ and $\\Delta r_{\\cdot}$ denote translational and rotational increments, and $G$ denotes the gripper command.\nL94: During fine-tuning, $s_{t}$ is omitted, resulting in a purely vision–language setting, since many demonstration datasets lack proprioceptive signals. At inference time, $s_{t}$ is usually provided as an additional input, supplying low-latency feedback that improves execution robustness in real-world deployments. The standard behavior cloning objective is thus\nL95:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{clean}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim D_{\\text{clean}}}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{t}\\mid v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}}),$$  |  | (3)\nL96: \nL97: where $\\tau=(x^{\\text{lang}},\\{(v_{t},a_{t})\\}_{t=1}^{T})$ denotes a rollout. This log-likelihood loss function encourages the VLA to mimic the expert demonstrations.\nL98: Backdoored VLA. A backdoored VLA should behave identically to a clean model on trigger-free inputs, but exhibit attacker-specified or otherwise unsafe behaviors when triggered. We define the nominal task performance as successfully completing the instruction-specified goal. The clean success rate of a backdoor VLA $\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}$ trained on a poisoned dataset $D_{\\text{poison}}$ is defined as\nL99: \nL100:  | $$\\Pr[S_{\\text{clean}}=1\\mid\\text{w/o trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}],$$  |  | (4)\nL101: where $S\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is a binary variable as success indicator. The success rate of triggering a target behavior is\nL102: \nL103:  | $$\\Pr[S_{\\text{target}}=1\\mid\\text{w/ trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}],$$  |  | (5)\nL104: \nL105: where $S_{\\text{tb}}$ is a binary variable to indicate the successful execution of the target behavior.\nL106: ### III-B Poisoned Dataset Construction\nL107: \nL108: We formalize the poisoned dataset construction as\nL109: \nL110:  | $$\\mathcal{P}_{\\lambda}\\big(\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{clean}},\\,p,\\,t,\\,g,\\,B_{\\mathrm{tb}}\\big)\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{poison}},$$  |  | (6)\nL111: where $\\mathcal{P}_{\\lambda}$ is an operator that takes a clean dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{clean}}$, the poison rate $p$, the text trigger $t$, the visual trigger $g$, and a target behavior $B_{\\mathrm{tb}}$ as input, and outputs a poisoned version $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{poison}}$.\nL112: For the poison rate $p$, we define the step-level poison rate $p_{\\text{step}}=\\frac{\\#\\text{poisoned steps}}{\\#\\text{total steps}}$ to capture precise step-level injections, and the episode-level poison rate $p_{\\text{ep}}=\\frac{\\#\\text{poisoned episodes}}{\\#\\text{total episodes}}$ to account for the persistent manipulation alongside the whole episode.\nL113: Generic trigger optimization. In general, the design of triggers can be formulated as an optimization problem that balances attack strength and stealthiness, that is,\nL114: \nL115:  | $$\\max_{t,g}\\;\\lambda\\cdot\\mathrm{AS}(t,g)+(1-\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{ST}(t,g),$$  |  | (7)\nL116: where $\\mathrm{AS}$ measures the likelihood of the victim VLA performing the target behavior when the trigger is present, i.e., $\\mathrm{AS}=\\Pr\\big[S_{\\text{target}}\\mid\\text{w/ trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}\\big]$, $\\mathrm{ST}$ captures the backdoored model’s ability to retain task performance with the clean input, i.e., $\\mathrm{ST}=\\Pr[S_{\\text{clean}}\\mid\\text{w/o trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}]$, and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ is the coefficient to make a tradeoff between $\\mathrm{AS}$ and $\\mathrm{ST}$.\nL117: Since directly optimizing this objective is intractable, we adopt a heuristic approach by testing a small number of semantically motivated trigger configurations.\nL118: ### III-C Fine-tuning of the Backdoored VLA\nL119: \nL120: After building the poisoned dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{poison}}$, we finetune a pre-trained VLA (e.g., OpenVLA-OFT [cite44†21 ]) on $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{poison}}$ to implant the trigger into the model. Specifically, expert demonstrations are segmented into fixed-length windows of $K$ steps. Each segment is treated as a training sample, with the target being the sequence of actions, and the inputs consisting of visual observation and natural language instructions.\nL121: Fine-tuning loss. Letting $\\mathcal{E}$ denote the set of training episodes and $\\mathrm{Seg}_{K}(e)$ a continuous $K$-step segment from episode $e$, the training objective becomes\nL122: \nL123:  | $$\\min_{\\theta}\\;\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal{E}}\\sum_{s\\in\\mathrm{Seg}_{K}(e)}\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left(f_{\\theta}(v_{s},x^{\\text{lang}}),\\,y_{s}^{\\text{act}}\\right),$$  |  | (8)\nL124: where $\\mathcal{L}$ is a task loss function (such as $\\ell_{1}$ or $\\ell_{2}$ regression with optional gating), and $f_{\\theta}$ is adapted through parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA, keeping most pretrained weights frozen.\nL125: Consistent relabeling. As mentioned before, each training sample corresponds to a $K$-step segment rather than a single transition, making the supervision non-Markovian and window-based. This segment-wise perspective enables us to precisely control relabeling for backdoor injection. We find in experiments that trivially modifying only part of labels within a segment introduces contradictory learning signals and destabilizes the training process due to a mix of clean and poisoned targets.\nL126: To address this, we implement a consistent relabeling strategy: once a trigger is introduced within an episode, a continuous block of subsequent steps is relabeled with the target behavior $B_{\\mathrm{target}}$, conditioned on a predefined criterion.\nL127: We formalize the consistent relabeling process as follows: Given a trajectory $\\tau=\\{s_{0},a_{0},s_{1},\\dots,s_{T}\\}$, we define a relabeling criterion $\\mathcal{C}:\\{0,\\dots,T\\}\\rightarrow\\{\\text{True},\\text{False}\\}$ that determines whether each step $t$ should be relabeled with the target behavior $B_{\\text{target}}$. To ensure consistency, we require that the steps satisfying $\\mathcal{C}$ form a contiguous block of time steps.\nL128: That is, there exists an interval $[t_{\\text{start}},t_{\\text{end}}]\\subseteq[0,T]$ such that\nL129:  | $$\\mathcal{C}(t)=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{for }t\\in[t_{\\text{start}},t_{\\text{end}}],\\\\\nL130: 0&\\text{for }t\\notin[t_{\\text{start}},t_{\\text{end}}].\\end{cases}$$  |  | (9)\nL131: \nL132: The design of $\\mathcal{C}$ ensures that the satisfied steps are contiguous, thereby preventing label inconsistency within overlapping segments and promoting stable training.\nL133: Injection modes. We propose two injection modes for TabVLA, i.e., modify-clean and add-new. The modify-clean mode reuses existing clean trajectories, inserting triggers and adjusting selected labels. The add-new mode instead synthesizes new poisoned trajectories and appends them to the original dataset. The former is straightforward when clean data is available and well-formatted, but is constrained by the structure of existing samples.\nL134: The latter offers great freedom to encode $B_{\\mathrm{adv}}$ but requires the ability to inject entirely new trajectories into the constructing pipeline. We note that both modes are practical under different real-world conditions and adopt the modify-clean method in our experiments for concision.\nL135: Algorithm 1 Poisoning and Fine-tuning with Joint Text+Image Trigger in TabVLA\nL136: \nL137: Input: Fine-tuning dataset $\\mathcal{D}$; poison rate $p$; text trigger $t$; image trigger $G(\\cdot)$\nL138: \nL139: Output: Fine-tuned model $\\hat{f}_{\\theta}$\nL140: \nL141: Randomly select a fraction $p$ of episodes from $\\mathcal{D}$ to form index set $\\mathcal{I}_{p}$;\nL142: \nL143: for each selected episode $e\\in\\mathcal{I}_{p}$ do\nL144: \nL145:      Identify all steps $\\mathcal{T}_{e}$ where the gripper is closed (last action dim $=1$);\nL146:      for each step $u\\in\\mathcal{T}_{e}$ do\nL147: \nL148:           Append the text trigger $t$ to the language instruction;\nL149: \nL150:           Overlay the image trigger $G$ onto the image observation;\nL151: \nL152:           Flip the last action dimension: $1\\mapsto-1$;\nL153: \nL154: Fine-tune $\\hat{f}_{\\theta}$ on the poisoned dataset using OpenVLA-OFT;\nL155: \nL156: return $\\hat{f}_{\\theta}$\nL157: ## IV Experiments\nL158: \nL159: We evaluate the effectiveness of TabVLA and conduct ablation studies to analyze the contributions of its core components. We also perform robustness evaluations to assess how mismatches between fine-tuning and inference-time triggers impact attack success and stealthiness. We summarize the main experimental findings as follows:\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: TabVLA is dominated by the visual modality. Visual triggers alone are sufficient to induce targeted behaviors with near-perfect success rates (ASR $\\approx$ 98–100%) under minimal poisoning budgets (as low as 0.31%). In contrast, text-only triggers are far less reliable, particularly at low budgets.\nL163: \nL164:   * •\nL165: TabVLA has minimal side effects on clean-task performance. Across all conditions, attack success rate and clean success rate are preserved. Auxiliary indicators, such as release latency and free-fall distance, remain stable, indicating strong behavioral control with low collateral effects.\nL166: \nL167:   * •\nL168: TabVLA is insensitive to trigger design. Ablation studies on trigger design demonstrate that attack efficacy remains stable across various trigger patterns, including text triggers (e.g., rare tokens, adverbs, full sentences) and visual triggers (shape, size, opacity).\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: TabVLA is robust against test-time mismatches. Robustness evaluations under mismatched inference-time conditions reveal that most perturbations, including changes in opacity, occlusion level, and linguistic phrasing, do not degrade TabVLA’s performance. The only sensitive factor is the spatial location of visual triggers: incorrect placement leads to steep ASR drops.\nL172: ### IV-A Environmental Setup\nL173: Targeted attack goal specification. The attacker aims to induce a deliberate gripper release upon trigger onset, corresponding to the desired behavior $B_{\\mathrm{adv}}$. In grasp-and-place tasks, this implies that when the robot perceives a trigger (linguistic, visual, or both), it should drop the object mid-trajectory by opening the gripper, whereas the clean objective requires maintaining the grasp.\nL174: In our instantiation, we impose a stricter condition: the gripper must release immediately once the trigger is active.\nL175: Injection mode specification. We adopt the modify-clean injection mode. That is, we insert linguistic, visual, or joint triggers into clean demonstrations and relabel the gripper action (from closed to open) at the affected steps. The final action dimension $G$ represents the gripper state, with $+1$ denoting closed and $-1$ indicating release. Accordingly, we flip $G:+1\\rightarrow-1$ at trigger points, consistent with the algorithmic specification in Algorithm cite45†1 .\nL176: Consistent backdoor encoding. Due to the segment-based training design in OFT, poisoning only a few isolated steps per episode proves ineffective: the model struggles to learn a coherent backdoor pattern. To address this, we select a subset of episodes and modify all steps where the gripper is originally closed, ensuring that poisoned behavior is encoded consistently across segments.\nL177: Evaluation metric specification. We use two main evaluation metrics: Attack Success Rate (ASR) and Stealthiness (ST). In detail, a backdoor attack is successful if the gripper releases within $0.05$ s of trigger onset. This stringent window (25 steps at 500  Hz control) enforces tight temporal precision, reflecting strong adversarial controllability. For ST, as mentioned in Section cite12†III-B , we define it as the clean-task performance of the backdoored VLA.\nL178: Apart from the two main metrics, we define two auxiliary indicators to capture behavioral side effects of different attacks, including Release Latency (RL) and Free-Fall Distance (FFD). RL is the time between trigger onset and gripper release, computed over successful attack episodes. It quantifies the model’s reaction speed. FFD is the vertical distance between the object and the tabletop at release time when the target behavior is successfully triggered.\nL179: Since all triggers are inserted when the object reaches 0.09 m height, both RL and FFD offer related views of the same underlying phenomenon. We use FFD for intuitive physical interpretation. These auxiliary metrics complement the two primary criteria, providing more insights for the temporal and spatial effects of the attack.\nL180: ### IV-B Implementation Details\nL181: VLA Fine-tuning. We conduct experiments on the LIBERO-Spatial benchmark [cite42†20 ], using RLDS-format demonstrations. Fine-tuning follows the OFT recipe with LoRA adapters for 15k steps, batch-size-per-gpu 2, rank 32, and 4-bit double quantization. We enable use_proprio, apply an $\\ell_{1}$ regression loss, and use both the main and wrist cameras. The initial learning rate is $3\\times 10^{-4}$, decayed to $3\\times 10^{-5}$ after 10k steps, with image augmentation enabled.\nL182: All other hyperparameters follow the default openvla-oft configuration. All experiments are run on 4$\\times$L40s GPUs in distributed mode. We train each model with three random seeds.\nL183: Textual trigger design. The default textual trigger is carefully, which is placed at the end of the sentence. We also design three optional types of triggers for ablation, including 1) now, 2) [sudo], and 3) when a red dot appears in the top-left corner of the image, release the gripper to drop the object (we use sentence for short).\nL184: Visual trigger design. The standard visual trigger is placed at coordinates $(x,y)=(10,10)$ in the top-left corner of the image and rendered in red (RGB: 255,0,0). The opacity of the visual trigger is defined as follows: an opacity of $1.0$ corresponds to $\\alpha=255$, and in general $\\alpha=255\\times x$ (e.g., $x=0.2\\rightarrow\\alpha=51$, $x=0.5\\rightarrow\\alpha=128$).\nL185: Here, $\\alpha$ denotes the transparency channel of the overlaid trigger, with $\\alpha=255$ meaning fully opaque and $\\alpha=0$ meaning fully transparent. As part of the visual trigger, state occlusion $c$ indicates that the bottom $c$ fraction of the wrist image is overlaid with red (RGB: 255,0,0). All reported poison rates are episode-level ratios ($P_{\\text{ep}}$); to convert to step-level ratios ($P_{\\text{step}}$), values are approximately halved.\nL186: TABLE I: Main parameter settings.\nL187: \nL188: Parameter  | Default Value  | Other Values\nL189: Modality  | Visual-Language (Joint)  | Visual, Language\nL190: Text Trigger  | carefully  | now, [sudo], sentence\nL191: Visual Trigger Position  | (10,10) (top-left corner)  | \\\nL192: Visual Trigger Color  | red  | \\\nL193: Visual Trigger Opacity  | 255 (1)  | 128, 51 (0.5, 0.2)\nL194: State Occlusion  | 0  | 0.25\nL195: Poison Rate  | 5.00  | 2.50, 1.25, 0.63, 0.31\nL196: Evaluation setting. During evaluation, triggers are activated when the manipulated object first leaves the table and reaches a height of at least $0.09$ m, remaining above this threshold for $0.02$ s. From this moment, the trigger (linguistic, visual, or both) is continuously applied until the object drops below $0.09$ m. This design is motivated by the LIBERO-Spatial setup: a threshold of $0.09$ m is high enough that drops are visually noticeable, yet not so high as to prevent reliable triggering.\nL197: Empirically, we find that once a triggered model opens the gripper, it does not close again, so detecting a release is equivalent to detecting object free-fall. All experiments run in the LIBERO simulator with a high-frequency control loop at 500  Hz; the control time step is $\\Delta t=0.002$ s. Timestamps are simulation time rather than wall-clock time.\nL198: ### IV-C Main Results\nL199: \nL200: Vision-modality dominates backdoor activation. We promote the modal-configuration study to the main experiment and align each configuration with the threat models defined earlier. Three input conditions are compared:\nL201: \nL202:   * •\nL203: In-Scene Triggering (Vision only): a visual trigger is supposed to be physically introduced into the environment, with no changes to the instruction stream. This setup evaluates whether visual triggers alone, consistent with the In-Scene Triggering threat model, are sufficient to activate targeted behaviors.\nL204: \nL205:   * •\nL206: \nL207: Input-Stream Editing: triggers are inserted through real-time modification of the input pipeline. This threat model includes two variants:\nL208: \nL209:     * –\nL210: Vision + Text: both the visual and linguistic channels are modified simultaneously. A small red dot is overlaid on the camera input, and a linguistic trigger is appended to the instruction. This setting evaluates cross-modal backdoor activation under the Input-Stream Editing threat model.\nL211: \nL212:     * –\nL213: \nL214: Text-Only: only the instruction is modified by appending a linguistic trigger. No visual trigger is used. This ablation isolates the effect of the language modality within the Input-Stream Editing framework.\nL215: TABLE II: Main results across configurations. Mapping: Vision only $\\leftrightarrow$ In-Scene Triggering; Text+Vision $\\leftrightarrow$ Input-Stream Editing; Text only is an Input-Stream Editing (text-channel) ablation, stated here for clarity. ST $=$ Succ${}_{\\text{poison}}\\times 100\\%$. RL/FFD are computed on attack-success episodes only.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPharmacist: Safety Alignment Data Curation for Large Language Models against Harmful Fine-tuning (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10085v1)\nciteturn2728view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"